course: dictionary
lesson: 674
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word bale is a good example. «Haymaking is cutting grass, drying it in the sun, turning it and baling it for winter» is a sentence about farm work, so baling here is a verb: pressing hay into bundles.
explain: A bale is a large, tightly packed bundle of hay, straw, cotton or wool, tied up with string or wire. Read the words around it. Cutting grass and drying it in the sun tell you the hay is being made ready for storing, so the farm clues point to the hay meaning.
explain: Always check your meaning. Try it in the sentence: «In June the grass is cut, dried in the sun, turned and baled as hay» makes sense if baled means pressed into bundles. If the sentence were about a plane or a boat, the clues would point to a different meaning of bale.
fact: A bale is a large, tightly packed bundle of hay, straw, cotton or wool.
fact: Bales are tied up with string or wire.
fact: As a verb, to bale hay means to press it into bales.
fact: In the UK, «bale out» can mean to jump out of a plane with a parachute.
fact: In the UK, «bale out» can also mean to scoop water out of a boat; in the US this is usually spelt «bail out».
exercise: «Haymaking is cutting grass, drying it in the sun, turning it and baling it for winter.» What is baling here? | Jumping out of a plane | Painting the barn red | Cutting the grass with a scythe | *Pressing hay into large, tightly packed bundles
exercise: «In June the grass is cut, dried in the sun, turned and baled as hay.» What does baled mean here? | *Pressed into bundles | Sheared like a sheep | Scooped out of a boat | Dried in a warm window
exercise: Which of these could be a bale? | *A bundle of cotton tied with string | A bicycle bell | A rain cloud | A glass of water
test: A pilot shouts, «Bale out!» In the UK, what does bale out mean? | To press hay into bundles | To wash the floor of a barn | *To jump out of a plane with a parachute | To tie a box with wire
test: «Damp hay packed in a barn can get hot and even catch fire.» Which word tells you the hay is in bales? | Damp | Hot | *Packed | Barn
exam: Ella reads «The farmer stacked a bale of hay in the barn.» What is a bale? | A kind of tractor | A type of sheep | A small empty box | *A large, tightly packed bundle of hay
